et Report 2026/27 Appendix 10","url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40145/budget-report-2026-27-appendix-10.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"10/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"Budget Report 2026/27 Appendix 11","url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40146/budget-report-2026-27-appendix-11.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"10/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"Budget Report 2026/27 Appendix 12","url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40147/budget-report-2026-27-appendix-12.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"10/02/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Strategy and Resources Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-15","committee":"Licensing and Planning Policy Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"East Surrey Shadow Authority Audit and Governance Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Strategy and Resources Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-24","committee":"Licensing and Planning Policy Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-01","committee":"Human Resources Panel","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary meeting","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Council","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-02","committee":"East Surrey Shadow Authority Audit and Governance Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Licensing and Planning Policy Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Epsom and Ewell halts property purchases after unitary authority announcement","lead":"Epsom and Ewell Council has halted its commercial property investment programme following October's government announcement that Surrey councils will be replaced by two new unitary authorities. The council budgeted \u00a349.6m for property purchases in 2025/26 but spent none, resulting in actual capital spending (money on buildings, roads and equipment) of \u00a32.8m. Despite tighter cash reserves\u2014down \u00a32.3m to \u00a327.8m\u2014the council's treasury management beat forecasts, delivering net investment income of \u00a31,178,313 against a mid-year prediction of \u00a31,042,000. Debt remained stable at \u00a364.4m.","iso":"2026-09-25","paper_title":"2025/26 Treasury Management Outturn","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s41920/41920.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s41981/41981.pdf","headline":"Council expresses concern that homelessness numbers may rise further with the onset of winter","detail":"The paper flags that homelessness numbers are expected to remain high and may rise further, particularly in winter when demand historically increases. This suggests the current overspend may worsen in the coming months.","category":"demand","matters":"Indicates the current overspend may worsen, and the council faces an ongoing budgeting challenge through the winter period.","figures":"None provided","quote":"Nevertheless, there is concern that homelessness numbers will remain high and possibly rise still further, particularly with the onset of winter, which historically sees increased demand.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s41981/41981.pdf","headline":"No new affordable housing was delivered in the past year, constraining the council's ability to move people on from temporary accommodation","detail":"The paper identifies lack of newly developed affordable housing as a root cause, noting zero units were delivered in the past year. With the private rental sector also unaffordable, this creates a structural constraint that may persist despite increased spending.","category":"demand","matters":"This identifies an underlying supply-side constraint that spending more on temporary accommodation services alone cannot solve. The problem may be structural, not operational.","figures":"No new affordable housing units delivered in past year","quote":"The review of service delivery , highlights that without access to sufficient affordable accommodation, and with much of the private rental sector remaining unaffordable, temporary accommodation (TA) usage may increase further.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s41981/41981.pdf","headline":"Council is allocating \u00a3750k growth budget and \u00a3128.5k in-year funding to temporary accommodation to address a structural deficit","detail":"The council is allocating \u00a3750k in growth funding for 2026/27 and an additional \u00a3128.5k in-year to temporary accommodation. The paper describes this as addressing a structural deficit in the service.","category":"overspend","matters":"The scale of allocation shows the council recognizes this as a major problem and is taking action to manage it, but also signals the problem is substantial and structural.","figures":"\u00a3750k growth budget for 2026/27; \u00a3128.5k in-year increase","quote":"It is worth noting that \u00a3750k growth budget was put into the nightly paid service for 2026/27 to address some of the structural deficit in this area . Furthermore, the budget has been further increased by \u00a3128.5k in year, funded from the inflationary provision.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s41981/41981.pdf","headline":"Homelessness applications have surged 86% in two years, with nightly paid accommodation numbers rising from approximately 20 to approximately 120","detail":"The paper shows homeless households in nightly paid accommodation rising from approximately 20 in April 2022 to approximately 120 by June 2026. Homelessness applications have risen each year, with recent years showing particularly steep increases.","category":"demand","matters":"A lender needs to assess whether this demand-driven growth is unsustainable or cyclical, and whether the council can continue to fund it. Demand has roughly doubled in 2 years.","figures":"86% rise in homelessness applications over two years; nightly paid accommodation rising from approximately 20 to approximately 120","quote":"The tables below show the total number of homeless households in nightly paid accommodation over the past four years and the huge rise (86%) in homelessness applications over the past two years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s41981/41981.pdf","headline":"Temporary accommodation service shows \u00a30.250m adverse variance in Q1 2026 due to elevated numbers of homeless households","detail":"The first-quarter forecast shows a \u00a30.250m overspend in temporary accommodation. This is driven by higher than expected numbers of homeless households requiring nightly paid accommodation.","category":"overspend","matters":"A material overspend in a demand-driven social care service that the council is legally obliged to fund, and one that continues to grow.","figures":"\u00a3 0.250m adverse variance","quote":"The updated forecast for  end June 2026 is a net adverse variance of \u00a3 0.250m, due to the continued elevated numbers of households in temporary accommodation (TA).","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-16"},"name":"Epsom & Ewell","findings_total":89,"news_drafts_total":1}}